summary:
  When a rebuild's thirty seconds run out its job number is retired on the
  spot, whether or not anything was sent after it.

history:
  It reads like the tidy version of the rule, and it is the wrong one. A
  rebuild empties the module list when it goes out, so if it is slow rather
  than dead and nothing was queued behind it, the loopbacks it loads are
  the only ones the room has. Treating its answer as a stranger's unloads
  every one of them: the group plays into a null sink nobody listens to
  until something else happens to ask for a rebuild. Only a newer job makes
  an older one stale.

target:
  package/contents/ui/SyncEngine.qml

find:
          _combineReloopBusy = false;
          // A rebuild queued behind the hung shell is the newest word on what

replace:
          _combineReloopBusy = false; _reloopJob++;
          // A rebuild queued behind the hung shell is the newest word on what

expect:
  Killed by tst_syncengine::test_a_hung_rebuild_with_no_successor_is_still_adopted_when_it_answers.
  Line-neutral.
